    "updated": "2026-10-17",
    "body": "# Ivan Illich\n\nPhilosopher and theologian (1926–2002) best known for his 1973 book *Tools for Conviviality* and his critique of Western technocratic institutions. Illich's foundational insight: technologies can be designed either to **enable autonomy** (convivial tools) or to **concentrate power** (manipulatory tools). \n\n## Convivial vs. Manipulatory Tools\n\nIllich distinguished between two classes of technology:\n\n**Convivial tools** are accessible, flexible, and noncoercive. Anyone can use them, as often or seldom as desired, for purposes of their own choosing. The public pay phone exemplifies this: you can say whatever you want to whoever you want, without bureaucratic mediation. Convivial tools foster self-realization.\n\n**Manipulatory tools** foreclose choice and require expert management. The automobile and highway system (Illich's prime example) reshape entire cities and value systems around themselves, devaluing alternatives like walking or public transit. Compulsory schooling and licensing systems similarly concentrate decision-making power in professional classes, devaluing people who haven't received credentials.\n\n## Detecting \"Murderous Logic\"\n\nIllich warned that even beneficial tools can cross a threshold where \"the means overtake the ends\" — what he called \"murderous logic.\" At that point, the tool rules its users rather than serving them. Technocracy, the rise of expert-managed systems, is a step toward fascism because it strips ordinary people of their capacity to act autonomously.\n\nHis solution: a research program to detect the early signs of murderous logic in tools and to design alternatives that maximize human liberty and enable self-realization.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[Convivial Tools]] — the core distinction Illich introduces\n- [[Technology Neutrality Debate]] — Illich cited as the counterpoint to determinism\n- [[Politics of Technical Artifacts]] — Winner's thesis on embedded power; Illich's framework adds how to evaluate tools' political structure\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[Technology Neutrality Debate]]"
