    "body": "# Small Language Models\n\nEfficient, compact AI models with millions to a few billion parameters—far fewer than the hundreds of billions in traditional large language models (LLMs). SLMs run locally on edge devices, work offline, and require minimal computational resources.\n\n## Characteristics\n\n- **Parameter range**: Millions to a few billion (vs. hundreds of billions for LLMs)\n- **Deployment**: Local, on-device execution\n- **Connectivity**: Work offline without cloud dependency\n- **Resource efficiency**: Minimal computational and energy requirements\n- **Specialization**: Optimized for specific, mission-critical tasks\n\n## Advantages over LLMs\n\n- **Local-first architecture** — align with [[Local-First Software]] principles\n- **Data privacy** — no cloud transmission required\n- **Cost efficiency** — reduced infrastructure and energy costs\n- **Independence** — no dependence on Big Tech platforms\n- **Environmental impact** — significantly lower resource consumption\n- **Reliability** — offline-capable, no internet dependency\n\n## Use Cases in [[Civic Tech]]\n\nSLMs are ideal for:\n- Specialized civic applications\n- Community-driven tools\n- Mission-critical government services\n- Accessible, offline-capable systems\n- Resource-constrained environments\n\n## Implementation\n\nExamples:\n- Hugging Face's SmolLM\n- Shakti SLM Series\n- IBM and Microsoft Azure SLM offerings\n\n## Connection to [[Smol Gardens]]\n\nThe Smol Gardens initiative specifically advocates for SLMs as an ethical alternative to LLMs in civic tech development, reducing environmental impact and maintaining community agency.\n\n## External References\n\n- \"Small Language Models are the Future of Agentic AI\" (NVIDIA, September 2025) — Economics and deployment architectures [[AI Economics and Deployment]]\n- Shakti SLM Series (March 2025) — Responsible AI principles in edge deployment [[Responsible AI Principles]]\n- IBM & Microsoft Azure documentation — SLM accessibility and responsible AI [[SLM Accessibility]]\n\n## SLM Philosophy & Practical Definition\n\nFor a timeless definition that doesn't depend on hardware specs that become obsolete, see [[SLM Philosophy and Definition]]. The core insight: intelligence is not defined by size alone but by the balance of **capability, efficiency, and context**. SLMs are compact enough to run on personal devices with low latency—making them the right tool for many agentic tasks, even if they're not the right tool for all tasks.\n\n## When to Use SLMs vs. LLMs\n\nThe choice between SLMs and LLMs depends on the specific task:\n- **70% viability** (Cradle GUI automation) — high repetition, clear patterns\n- **60% viability** (MetaGPT software development) — specialized task decomposition\n- **40% viability** (Open Operator workflow automation) — requires flexible reasoning\n\nSee [[SLM vs LLM Tradeoffs]] for detailed analysis of when each model class is appropriate.\n\n## Case Studies: SLM Replacement in Real Agentic Systems\n\n- [[MetaGPT Agent Case Study]] — 60% of queries replaceable in multi-agent software development\n- [[Open Operator Case Study]] — 40% of queries replaceable in workflow automation\n- [[Cradle GUI Agent Case Study]] — 70% of queries replaceable in GUI automation\n\nThese case studies reveal that across different domains, 40-70% of LLM queries can be replaced with appropriately specialized SLMs, yielding 10-30x cost savings while maintaining quality.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[SLM Philosophy and Definition]] — foundational reasoning about scale and intelligence\n- [[SLM vs LLM Tradeoffs]] — when each is appropriate\n- [[Accountable AI]] — responsible AI development\n- [[Local-First Software]] — local deployment and data control\n- [[Environmental Impact of AI]] — resource efficiency\n- [[Civic Tech]] — technology for democratic purposes\n- [[AI Economics and Deployment]] — economic and architectural considerations\n- [[Responsible AI Principles]] — ethical AI deployment\n- [[SLM Accessibility]] — making SLMs accessible and usable"
